CHUNK({"chunk":"3a","module":"m3","topics":[{"id":"m3-intro","title":"Engineering drawing: importance, scope and standards","short":"Intro and standards","blurb":"Why engineers draw, sketch vs engineering drawing, where drawings are used, and the ASME, ISO and BIS standards.","source":"Lecture 1, slides 2–6","tabs":[{"title":"Communicating a design","html":"<p class=\"lead\">An idea travels to the factory through a chain of channels: <b>oral → written → sketch → engineering drawing → 3D model/CAD → manufacturing</b>. Each step is more precise than the one before.</p>\n<ul><li>A <b>technical drawing</b> is a set of drawings that communicate an idea, design, schematic or model.</li><li>An <b>engineering drawing</b> is a type of technical drawing used to <b>fully and clearly define the requirements</b> for an engineered part or system.</li><li>It gives the <b>shape description, dimensions, tolerancing, annotations, parts list</b> and everything else needed for manufacturing and quality control.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>Engineering drawing is the universal language of engineers: it reduces errors in manufacturing and keeps a permanent record for reference and documentation.</p></div>"},{"title":"Sketch vs drawing","html":"<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Sketch</th><th>Engineering drawing</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Capture an idea</td><td>Communicate a finished design</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Execution</td><td>Freehand, fast</td><td>Precise instruments or CAD</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>Approximate, rough</td><td>Exact dimensions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tools</td><td>Paper, pencil, pen</td><td>Drafting board, CAD software</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Audience</td><td>Self, team, concept stage</td><td>Manufacturing, QC, legal, vendors</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Time</td><td>Minutes (2–15 min)</td><td>Hours or days</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Legal document?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (binding agreement)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Skill</td><td>Basic drawing skill</td><td>Professional drafting standards</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A sketch is a quickly executed freehand drawing that is <b>not</b> meant to be a drawing of the finished part.</p>"},{"title":"Steps to a part","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Role of the drawing in manufacturing, in seven steps:</p>\n<ol><li><b>Design</b>, on paper or CAD</li><li><b>Drawing</b>, with all views and dimensions</li><li><b>Shape description</b>, communicated precisely to the manufacturer</li><li><b>Dimensions</b> for manufacturing</li><li><b>Tolerancing</b>: the allowable deviation</li><li><b>Annotations and notes</b>: material, finish, specifications</li><li><b>Manufacturing and QC</b>: inspect the part against the drawing</li></ol>\n<h4>One language, many branches</h4>\n<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Mechanical</span><b>Part drawings, assemblies, machine components, 3D CAD and GD&amp;T</b></div><div><span>Civil</span><b>Site plans, structural drawings, sections, infrastructure layouts</b></div><div><span>Electrical</span><b>Wiring diagrams, panel layouts, single-line diagrams, power systems</b></div><div><span>Electronics</span><b>PCB layouts, schematics, component placement, embedded systems</b></div></div>"},{"title":"ASME, ISO, BIS","html":"<p class=\"lead\"><b>Standards</b> are a set of rules controlling how an engineering drawing is presented.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>ASME (USA)</th><th>ISO (worldwide)</th><th>BIS (India)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Projection</td><td>Third angle</td><td>First or third, by country</td><td><b>First angle</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Units</td><td>Inches</td><td>mm</td><td>mm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sheet sizes</td><td>ASME Y14.1</td><td>ISO 5457 (A0–A5)</td><td><b>IS 10711:2001</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lines</td><td>ASME Y14.2</td><td>ISO 128-20</td><td><b>IS 10714</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dimensioning</td><td>ASME Y14.5</td><td>ISO 129</td><td>IS 11669</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Why standards help: fewer reading, manufacturing and inspection mistakes; international communication; uniform conventions; legal and contract clarity; better efficiency and quality; lower errors and cost.</p>\n<div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The source slide gives the ISO sheet standard as ISO 5455. ISO 5455 is about <b>scales</b>; sheet sizes and layout are ISO 5457 (with ISO 216 for the A sizes). BIS is the national standards body of India and its use is mandatory in government projects.</p></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Define an engineering drawing.","back":"A technical drawing that fully and clearly defines the requirements for an engineered part or system."},{"front":"Sketch vs engineering drawing: which is a legal document?","back":"The engineering drawing (a binding agreement); a sketch is not."},{"front":"What does an engineering drawing give the manufacturer?","back":"Shape description, dimensions, tolerancing, annotations, parts list: everything needed to make and inspect the part."}],"cardCount":7,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m3-sheets","title":"Drawing sheets, title block and scales","short":"Sheets and scales","blurb":"A-series sheet sizes, the layout of a sheet with margins and title block, and full, reduced and enlarged scales.","source":"Lecture 1, slides 15–20","tabs":[{"title":"Sheet sizes","html":"<p class=\"lead\">BIS sheet sizes follow <b>IS 10711:2001</b>. A0 is about 1 m²; each next size is the previous one <b>cut in half across its long side</b>, so all sizes have sides in the ratio 1 : √2.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m3-sheet-sizes\"></figure>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Trimmed (mm)</th><th>Untrimmed (mm)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>A0</td><td>841 × 1189</td><td>880 × 1230</td></tr><tr><td>A1</td><td>594 × 841</td><td>625 × 880</td></tr><tr><td>A2</td><td>420 × 594</td><td>450 × 625</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A3</td><td>297 × 420</td><td>330 × 450</td></tr><tr><td>A4</td><td>210 × 297</td><td>240 × 330</td></tr><tr><td>A5</td><td>148 × 210</td><td>165 × 240</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>"},{"title":"Sheet layout","html":"<figure data-fig=\"m3-sheet-layout\"></figure>\n<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Border</span><b>Outer boundary of the sheet</b></div><div><span>Inner frame</span><b>Defines the working area</b></div><div><span>Margins</span><b>Space for handling and filing: a = 30 mm on the binding edge, b = 20 mm on the others</b></div><div><span>Title block</span><b>170 × 65 mm, bottom right; holds the drawing details</b></div><div><span>Centre marks</span><b>For positioning and scanning</b></div><div><span>Trim marks</span><b>Show the final cut size</b></div><div><span>Zone references</span><b>Coordinates for locating details</b></div></div>\n<div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The A3 layout figure on slide 16 uses smaller margins (20 mm on the filing side, 5 mm on the others). Use a = 30, b = 20 and the 170 × 65 title block (slide 18) unless your teacher says otherwise.</p></div>"},{"title":"Title block","html":"<p class=\"lead\">The title block (170 × 65 mm) sits in the bottom-right corner of the drawing area.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Fields</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Company info</td><td>Company or college name, department, logo, address</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drawing info</td><td>Title of drawing, subject, scale, dimension unit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Document info</td><td>Drawing number, sheet size, sheet \"1 of 1\", revision number and date</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Personal info</td><td>Drawn by, checked by, approved by (name, date, signature)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Standards</td><td>Projection symbol, tolerance, material, BIS/ISO marks</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>"},{"title":"Scales","html":"<div class=\"eq\" data-label=\"Scale\">Scale = drawing size : actual size</div>\n<figure data-fig=\"m3-scale-demo\"></figure>\n<div data-viz=\"scale-calc\"></div>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Examples</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Full size</td><td>1 : 1</td><td>Object fits comfortably on the sheet (a 50 × 30 plate)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enlarged</td><td>2 : 1, 5 : 1</td><td>Small parts: an M3 screw at 2 : 1, a Ø8 gear at 5 : 1 (drawn Ø40)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reduced</td><td>1 : 2, 1 : 5, 1 : 10, 1 : 100</td><td>Large parts and buildings: a 1000 mm shaft at 1 : 10 is drawn 100 mm; a 20 000 mm house at 1 : 100 is drawn 200 mm</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"given\"><p>A plummer block base is 250 mm long. Draw it at 1 : 5.</p></div><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">scale factor = 1/5 = 0.2</div><div class=\"eq\">drawn length = 250 × 0.2 = 50 mm</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The scale slides write the <b>drawn</b> length on the reduced drawing (e.g. 50 mm) to show the shrink. On a real drawing you always write the <b>true</b> size, whatever the scale, and state the scale in the title block.</p></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Trimmed size of A0? Of A3?","back":"A0: 841 × 1189 mm. A3: 297 × 420 mm."},{"front":"How do you get A1 from A0?","back":"Cut A0 in half across its long side: 594 × 841."},{"front":"Ratio of the sides of any A sheet?","back":"1 : √2."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":2},{"id":"m3-cad","title":"Drawing instruments and introduction to CAD","short":"Instruments and CAD","blurb":"The manual drafting instruments and what each is for, then manual drafting compared with CAD.","source":"Lecture 1, slides 21–24","tabs":[{"title":"Instruments","html":"<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Scale ruler</td><td>Measuring and reading scales (e.g. a 1 : 100 plan)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compass</td><td>Circles and arcs (e.g. a 25 mm radius circle)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dividers</td><td>Transferring measurements and stepping off equal distances</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Set squares</td><td>45° set square: 45°, vertical and horizontal lines. 30°/60° set square: 30°, 60° and their complements</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Protractor</td><td>Measuring and setting angles (e.g. a 75° line from a baseline)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>T-square</td><td>Horizontal lines; slide it down for parallel lines; guide for set squares</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mini drafter</td><td>Parallel, perpendicular and angled lines quickly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>French curves</td><td>Smooth irregular curves (airfoil, ergonomic profile)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Templates</td><td>Repeated standard shapes: circles, symbols</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Pencils: <b>HB for thick lines</b>, <b>2H for thin lines</b> (Lecture 2, slide 3).</p>"},{"title":"Manual vs CAD","html":"<p class=\"lead\"><b>CAD</b> (computer-aided design/drafting) draws the same part on screen with software such as AutoCAD, SolidWorks, CATIA or Fusion 360.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Manual</th><th>CAD</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Tools</td><td>Manual instruments</td><td>Computer and software</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed</td><td>Slower</td><td>Faster</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>Moderate, skill-based</td><td>High, software-assisted</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Modifications</td><td>Difficult</td><td>Easy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Storage</td><td>Physical sheets</td><td>Digital files</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Collaboration</td><td>Limited</td><td>Easy sharing and teamwork</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Repetition</td><td>Time-consuming</td><td>Quick copy and reuse</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Industry use</td><td>Limited today</td><td>Widely used</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>"},{"title":"What CAD adds","html":"<ul><li>Faster editing, duplication and revision.</li><li>Higher precision and standardisation.</li><li>Easy storage, sharing, scaling and printing.</li><li>Supports <b>2D drafting and 3D modelling</b>; tools such as <b>layers</b> and a <b>grid</b>.</li><li>Outputs: digital files, PDFs, <b>CNC-ready data</b>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>Manual drafting builds the core skills and visualisation; CAD improves speed, accuracy, revision control and productivity. The standards (lines, projection, dimensioning) are the same in both.</p></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which instrument steps off equal distances?","back":"Dividers."},{"front":"Angles available with the two set squares?","back":"45° set square: 45°; 30°/60° set square: 30° and 60° (plus vertical and horizontal lines)."},{"front":"What is a French curve for?","back":"Drawing smooth irregular curves."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[{"id":"m3-sheet-sizes","src":"m3/pages/l1-16.png","crop":null,"caption":"A-series sheets: each size is the previous one halved across its long side.","alt":"A0 sheet divided into A1, A2, A3, A4 and A5 with a table of trimmed sizes."},{"id":"m3-sheet-layout","src":"m3/pages/l1-18.png","crop":null,"caption":"Layout of an A2 sheet: 30 mm filing margin, 20 mm other margins, 170 × 65 title block at bottom right.","alt":"A2 sheet with inner frame, margins a and b, and a title block in the bottom-right corner."},{"id":"m3-scale-demo","src":"m3/pages/l1-20.png","crop":null,"caption":"Reduced scale 1 : 2: every length on the drawing is half the real length.","alt":"An L bracket drawn at full size and at half size with 100 and 50 dimension lines."}]});
